    "chunk_text": "In the retail market companies that mainly contributed to the turnover were; Browns Investments Rs 307 million (37.8 million shares traded), LB Finance Rs 219 million (1.5 million shares traded), JKH Rs 212 million (9.4 million shares traded), NTB Rs 211 million (669,000 shares traded), Vallibel One Rs 186 million (1.8 million shares traded), PickMe Rs 166 million (1.2 million shares traded) and Sierra Cables Rs 160 million (5.7 million shares traded). During the day 185.8 million share volumes changed hands in 31214 transactions.\n\nMercantile Investments and Finance said it will seek shareholder approval for a share sub-division which will see an ordinary share split into 200 ordinary shares. 3,006,000 ordinary shares of the company are to be split, CSE sources said. There will be 601,200,000 ordinary shares after the subdivision. The subdivision of shares will not result in any change to the stated capital of the company, Mercantile Investments and Finance said.\n\nYesterday the rupee opened slightly weaker at Rs 302.55/60 to the US dollar, from Rs 302.45/60 the previous day, while bond yields were broadly steady, dealers said.\n\nA bond maturing on 15.12.2026 was quoted at 8.25/35 percent, down from 8.28/33 percent. A bond maturing on 15.09.2027 was quoted flat at 8.65/75 percent. A bond maturing on 15.12.2028 was quoted at 9.00/9.05 percent, down from 9.05/15 percent. A bond maturing on 01.07.2030 was quoted at 9.70/75 percent, up from 9.68/75 percent. A bond maturing on 15.12.2032 was quoted at 10.40/50 percent, up from 10.42/48 percent.\n\nAn auction of Rs. 38,000 million Treasury bills was ongoing. The telegraphic transfer rates for the American dollar was 299.0000 buying, 306.0000 selling; the British pound was 403.0983 buying and 414.4601 selling, and the euro was 350.6526 buying, 362.0158 selling.\n\nComBank wins impressive triple at ICC Emerging Asia Banking Conclave & Awards 2025"
