    "md5": "003ed7b97a0810d0d5cd460a4a4a7efe",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1839,
    "chunk_text": "Probes and politics\n\nOpposition Leader Sajith Premadasa has requested the government to ensure that the ongoing investigation into the 2019 Easter Sunday terror attacks, which snuffed out about 275 lives, will be conducted in a transparent and credible manner. Making a special statement in Parliament, on Monday, he pointed out that a person whose child was killed in the terror attacks had written to the UNHRC, expressing his dissatisfaction with the probe. The government ought to heed the Opposition\u2019s call.\n\nOne wonders whether a pang of conscience has prompted Premadasa to make the aforesaid request. He and several other SJB seniors were in the Cabinet of the UNP-led Yahapalana government, which failed to prevent the Easter Sunday carnage despite the availability of actionable intelligence. The Presidential Commission of Inquiry which probed the Easter Sunday attacks has said, inter alia: \u201cThe dysfunctional government was a major contributory factor for the events that took place on 21st April 2019.\u201d (pp. 470-71) The JVP also backed that administration, which shielded the corrupt and neglected national security.\n\nThe SJB seniors cannot absolve themselves of responsibility for the Yahapalana government\u2019s lapses that led to the Easter Sunday attacks, as we argued in a previous editorial comment. It is hoped that the Opposition, led by the SJB, will get off its high horse and remain intensely focused on the Easter Sunday carnage probe.\n\nThe ongoing investigation into the Easter Sunday attacks is widely considered emblematic because it is expected to delve into a host of crucial issues, such as systemic failures in intelligence and the State\u2019s inability to ensure expeditious delivery of justice. Several negative factors have taken their toll on the current probe, the main being a conflict of interest."
  },
