    "chunk_text": "Of that insidious evil\n\nPresident Anura Kumara Dissanayake, addressing the UN General Assembly on Thursday, called for a concerted global effort to eliminate the evil of corruption. He rightly described corruption as a destructive force that undermined development, democracy and social wellbeing while perpetuating poverty. He noted that Sri Lanka had already embarked on a difficult but essential campaign to eliminate corruption for the sake of future generations and urged all countries to do so. He flaunted his government\u2019s anti-corruption drive as a success story. Whether other nations will heed his call and join forces to launch a global-scale war on corruption remains to be seen. However, one cannot but agree with President Dissanayake on the need to do everything possible to rid Sri Lanka of the twin evils of bribery and corruption to achieve economic recovery and national progress.\n\nClose on the heels of President Dissanayake\u2019s clarion call, in New York, for battling corruption with might and main to make the world a better place, Chairman of the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption, Neil Iddewela, in Colombo, shed light on public perception of state sector corruption in this country. Speaking at a seminar\u2013\u2013\u201cIntegrity, Transparency, and Good Governance for National Development\u201d\u2013\u2013jointly organised by the Sri Lanka Administrative Service Association and the Sri Lanka Institute of Administrative Professionals, Iddawela made public some findings of a countrywide survey his institution had conducted to ascertain public perception of corruption, as part of its national anti-corruption action plan."
