    "chunk_text": "Close on the heels of President Dissanayake\u2019s clarion call, in New York, for battling corruption with might and main to make the world a better place, Chairman of the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption, Neil Iddewela, in Colombo, shed light on public perception of state sector corruption in this country. Speaking at a seminar\u2013\u2013\u201cIntegrity, Transparency, and Good Governance for National Development\u201d\u2013\u2013jointly organised by the Sri Lanka Administrative Service Association and the Sri Lanka Institute of Administrative Professionals, Iddawela made public some findings of a countrywide survey his institution had conducted to ascertain public perception of corruption, as part of its national anti-corruption action plan.\n\nMeanwhile, staggering losses that the country suffers due to politicians\u2019 corrupt practices are believed to be incalculable, for they have taken a heavy toll on foreign investment and led to massive cost overruns in development projects. There have also been instances where some foreign-funded projects came to a halt allegedly due to politicians\u2019 demand for bribes. These allegations have gone uninvestigated. Former French President Nicolas Sarkozy has been sentenced to five years in jail for a criminal conspiracy to raise campaign funds from Libya in 2007. That some Sri Lankan politicians and political parties receive funds from foreign sources is public knowledge. There is said to be no such thing as a free lunch, and campaign funds come with strings attached. But no Sri Lankan politician has been prosecuted for undisclosed campaign financing."
