    "md5": "c4cd79bcb0570e9dfe7e21ccb8c19d51",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1989,
    "chunk_text": "Events \u2013 Local health fairs, walks, public talks, podcasts, fundraisers and sporting events, cardiovascular screenings or seminars that focus on cardiovascular health and preventive strategies.\n\nCardiovascular disease is a combination of diseases in heart and blood vessels.\n\nIn WHO South East region, for instance, CVD account for 3.9 million deaths annually, nearly half of these are before age 70.\n\nWe request people to take charge of their heart health by taking the following steps to reduce risk of CVD:\n\nStopping smoking at home will improve children\u2019s heart health and also it dramatically reduces the risk of heart attacks, strokes, and sudden death. Strong motivation will need to quit smoking. Medical assistance will also help to support this.\n\nUnhealthy diet is the cause of many health issues, especially obesity, diabetes and cardiovascular diseases. Rapid urbanisation, changing the lifestyle and easy access to fast foods have made dietary patterns unhealthy.\n\nTrans fats are the worst type of fat we can eat. It reduces HDL-C (good cholesterol) and raises LDL cholesterol (bad cholesterol). This can make heart attacks or strokes more likely. Most trans fats are formed through a manufacturing process adding hydrogen to vegetable oils, which causes the oil to become solid at room temperature.\n\nProcessed foods like baked (cookies, cakes and crackers), fried food (French fries, doughnuts), muffins, sausages, non-dairy creamer, burgers, pizza, margarine spread or peanut butter, and any type of deep-fried foods carry trans fats.\n\nEg: Olive, peanuts, sunflower, and canola oil, avocado, almond, walnuts, and other nuts, fatty fish.\n\nModerate physical activity like brisk walking and gardening for at least 150 minutes per week with muscle strengthening activities two days a week.\n\nUse a smaller plate \u2013 eating a small portion with a single serving.\n\nexcessive drinking can lead to high blood pressure, heart diseases, strokes, liver disease, and various cancers."
