    "chunk_text": "Moderate physical activity like brisk walking and gardening for at least 150 minutes per week with muscle strengthening activities two days a week.\n\nUse a smaller plate \u2013 eating a small portion with a single serving.\n\nexcessive drinking can lead to high blood pressure, heart diseases, strokes, liver disease, and various cancers.\n\nElevation of blood pressure, and release of stress hormones (adrenaline) which can increase risk of heart disease, especially in women. Exercise, deep breathing, and relaxing your muscles and meditation, adequate sleep, various hobbies, listening to music, talking about your problem with your close friends, and being with your family and friends making time for the things you love are some facts to help manage stress levels.\n\nAsian specific cutoffs are lower because they are more predictive of health risks such as diabetes and cardiovascular disease in Asian population compared to the global standard.\n\n\u200b(Global standards define overweight as a BMI of 25-29.9 kg/m\u00b2 and obesity > 30 kg/m\u00b2)\n\n\u200bIn Asian regions (India and Sri Lanka) BMI of 23 kg/m\u00b2 is considered the threshold for overweight with normal range from 18.5 to 22.9 kg/m\u00b2.\n\n\u200bIn Sri Lanka, underweight is <18.5 kg/m\u00b2, normal 18.5-22.9 kg/m\u00b2, overweight/obesity is >23 kg/m\u00b2.\n\nKnow your Blood pressure which is a risk factor for CVD. Check regularly and control it with medication and exercise.\n\nKnow your cholesterol \u2013 High cholesterol is another major risk factor for CVD. It should be checked regularly and control with dietary measures and medication.\n\nKnow your Blood sugar \u2013 Diabetes is a very high risk for CVD. Diet control and medication required to control it.\n\nRecognising symptoms of CVD can help you survive because earlier the treatment better the chance of survival."
  },
