    "md5": "a6a40dfe012b1667935d40a24ac4fc2a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1908,
    "chunk_text": "Know your Blood sugar \u2013 Diabetes is a very high risk for CVD. Diet control and medication required to control it.\n\nRecognising symptoms of CVD can help you survive because earlier the treatment better the chance of survival.\n\n(b) Sudden weakness of limbs, slurring of speech, deviating of mouth to a side, numbness of face, limbs, or double vision could be due to a stroke. Knowing their symptoms and seeking medical assistance allows you to get treatment early and prevent complication which can be life-threatening most of the time.\n\nIf you are already diagnosed with a Heart disease or stroke, taking your medicine without fail will reduce the chance of getting another attack of Stroke or Heart disease.\n\nBreast feeding is the best form of nutrition for new born and infants to have lower cholesterol and Blood pressure as well as lower rate of overweight and obesity all of which improve cardiac health.\n\nEvidence suggest that new borns who are undernourished while in uterus bear a higher CVD risk later in life.\n\nMaternal obesity during pregnancy has been associated with obesity in children which increases risk of CVD in later life.\n\nBy doing these household steps above, you and your family can reduce burden of heart disease and strokes.\n\nPeople all over the world can have longer and better life style through prevention and control of heart disease and stroke.\n\nWorld Heart day\u2019s theme \u201cDon\u2019t Miss a Beat\u201d is not just inspirational slogans \u2013 they are calls to urgent concrete actions with the right policies, community support, health system capacity and individual commitment.\n\nStay happy and healthy, warm wishes on World Heart Day for a heart that only knows love, laughter and longevity\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
