    "md5": "c9b3865044038b008e7739d002305ec8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1990,
    "chunk_text": "Any unsterilized transfer of dollars to the Treasury, kills rupee liquidity (deflationary policy), slows credit, helps create a current account surplus (or narrow a deficit) and allows the central bank to buy more dollars to finance outflows or build reserves.\n\nAnalysts have also warned that the \u2018buffer\u2019 to repay debt is not the past collections of reserves as claimed by the statistical \u2018ARA metrics\u2019 of the IMF, but the interest rates as an ongoing tool in the present and future.\n\nAt the moment however deflationary policy is limited to interest coupons on the central bank bond portfolio.\n\nMeanwhile bank interest rates have also edged up a little, which will boost savings, allow the central bank to collect reserves or repay debt.\n\nRELATED : Sri Lanka bank interest rates edge up amid \u2018ramrod anomaly\u2019 in bills\n\nA stable exchange (implies inflation and currency destruction close to that suffered by US citizens) will allow capital to be preserved and helping an eventual fall in the interest rate.\n\nClassical economists have warned that interest rates are not determined by central bank reserve money (OMO) but the capital of a country.\n\n\u201cIt can, I think, be made manifest, that the rate of interest is not regulated by the abundance or scarcity of money, but by the abundance or scarcity of that part of capital, not consisting of money,\u201d Adam Smith pointed out.\n\nBefore the central bank was set up, Sri Lanka was raising 20 year money around 3 percent.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s rupee has depreciated in 2025, despite the record current account surplus, showing that the Mercantilist narrative spread by macro-economists that external current account deficits lead to depreciation (and not the flawed operating framework of a central bank) is false.\n\nRELATED : Sri Lanka rupee depreciates in July amid $244mn current account surplus\n\nHowever it is a useful narrative since it allows macro-economists to escape accountability and perpetuate inflationism. (Colombo/Oct01/2025)"
