    "md5": "f778a778c6a30706a1ebaee79f4d3e01",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1955,
    "chunk_text": "Colombo Stock Exchange receives \u2018Great Place To Work\u2019 certification for 4th consecutive year\n\nThe Colombo Stock Exchange (CSE) announces that it has received the \u2018Great Place To Work\u2019 certification for the 4th consecutive year in a row, having received the certification in 2022, 2023, 2024 and now once again in 2025. This prestigious certification underscores the CSE\u2019s consistent commitment to fostering a welcoming, inclusive and safe environment.\n\n\u201cAt CSE, our people are our greatest strength.\u201d stated Rajeeva Bandaranaike CEO of the CSE, \u201cWe\u2019ve worked hard to foster a workplace where fairness, respect, and pride thrive \u2014 and earning the Great Place to Work Certification for the fourth time in a row is a reflection of that commitment. It\u2019s a celebration of the culture we\u2019ve built together.\u201d\n\nGreat Place to Work\u00ae, the organization that awarded the certification, has set the standard in over 180+ countries and regions and represents over 20 million employees and 22,000 companies.\n\nEarning this certification for the 4th consecutive year demonstrates CSE\u2019s steadfast commitment to its core values: Professionalism, Integrity, Care, Passion and Teamwork. The CSE\u2019s advocacy for equality and inclusivity has produced a stable, welcoming and sustainable workplace for its employees and cultivated a work culture that respects diversity and promotes equal opportunities for its employees irrespective of race, gender or creed.\n\nThis steady stride to create a discrimination-free workplace remains a top priority for CSE as it fulfils its commitment to better representation of women, having employed women far above the market average.\n\nThe survey to award the certification is undertaken anonymously amongst employees with a rubric composed of five elements \u2013 credibility, respect, fairness, pride and camaraderie. In the survey CSE scored highly, with 94% of employees finding CSE offers a safe work environment."
