    "chunk_size_bytes": 1739,
    "chunk_text": "Crime and Sri Lankan politics are conjoined twins. Since 1977, when violence came to be institutionalised, all governments have been led by political parties with criminal records. The UNP\u2019s 17-year rule (1977 to 1994) began with the release of a notorious underworld figure, Gonawala Sunil, who had been serving a jail term for raping a teenage girl, from prison on a presidential pardon. The UNP had a powerful underworld army, which attacked the Opposition, suppressed democratic dissent and rigged elections; one of its members, Kalu Lucky, stoned the houses of the Supreme Court judges who dared stand up to President J. R. Jayewardene. Soththi Upali, another underworld figure working for the UNP, terrorised not only ordinary people but also the police, with top cops saluting him in public. Presidents Jayewardene and Ranasinghe Premadasa institutionalised political violence and shielded criminal gangs openly.\n\nThe Yahapalana government consisted of the UNP and the SLFP\u2014two parties with criminal records. The SLPP is an off-shoot of the SLFP, led by the Rajapaksas, but its government could not flex its underworld muscles because it ruined the economy, causing its leaders to head for the hills, with irate people in close pursuit. The incumbent government is led by the JVP, which committed many crimes, such as murders including political assassinations, armed robberies and arson attacks on public property. One of its hitmen, Lionel Ranasinghe, arrested by the CID confessed to as many as 41 murders including the assassination of Vijaya Kumaratunga during the JVP\u2019s second uprising. His shocking confession has been reproduced verbatim in Dharman Wickremeratne\u2019s book, Comrade Lionel. He was killed by his captors."
  },
